# Implementation from Spec (Plan Then Execute; Reconcile Docs)

When implementing a feature from a spec:

## Prerequisites
- Spec is committed (`spec:` prefix)
- Tests are committed (`test:` prefix) and currently failing
- Pre-implementable docs are committed (`docs:` prefix) — if the spec's Documentation Pre-implementable section lists any. (If not applicable, no docs commit required.)

## Phase 1: Plan (present before writing any code)
1. Run `git diff` against the spec, test, and docs commits to see scope
2. Run tests — confirm they fail (TDD "red" phase)
3. Read the full spec — every filled section (Security, Accessibility, Privacy, Performance, Observability, Deployment, etc., not just Functional)
4. Read committed pre-implementable docs (admin guides, API contracts, etc.) — they describe how the feature is intended to be used and should drive implementation thinking
5. Read existing code to match patterns
6. Present the **implementation plan** to the user:
   - Which files will be created or modified
   - What each change does
   - Which failing test(s) each change addresses
   - Which doc claims each change satisfies (or which docs may need reconciliation)
   - Any architectural decisions or trade-offs
7. Wait for approval before proceeding

## Phase 2: Execute (after plan approval)
1. Write code following the approved plan
2. Run tests — they should all pass (TDD "green" phase)
3. **Reconcile docs with reality** — re-read committed docs. Update any claims that turned out inaccurate (renamed field, edge case discovered, UX adjustment). This is normal, not exceptional.
   - Small adjustments: fold into the `feat:` commit, call out in the message body
   - Meaningful revisions: separate `docs:` commit before the `feat:` commit
4. Self-review: does each new/changed AC have corresponding code? Does the implementation address requirements from every filled spec section? Are docs accurate?

## Rules
- **Plan first, execute after approval** — never write code without an approved plan
- Build exactly what the spec says — no more, no less
- The tests define "done" — when all tests pass, implementation is complete
- Match existing patterns in neighboring code
- Never weaken a test to make it pass — fix the implementation instead
- Never ship code that contradicts committed docs without updating the docs
- For modifications: preserve existing behavior where the spec didn't change
